 — tu tinha razão: **é validação local** O breakpoint parou exatamente no `ws2_32!getpeername` (19 hits). Desassemblei a cadeia toda: **1) `Main.dll+0xBB170` = `bool IsGameServerSocket(SOCKET s)`** — o cliente **valida o peer localmente**: ```asm 0x6E88B192: call [getpeername] 0x6E88B19B: jz 0x6E88B1D2 ; erro → xor al,al (retorna 0) 0x6E88B1A6: mov ecx, 0xB34D ; 45901 ← PORTA REAL DO GS 0x6E88B1AB: cmp ax, cx 0x6E88B1AE: jb 0x6E88B1D2 ; porta < 45901 → FALHA 0x6E88B1B9: mov ecx, 0xDA8E ; 55950 0x6E88B1BE: cmp ax, cx 0x6E88B1C1: setbe al ; retorno = (porta ∈ [45901..55950]) ``` **2) `Main.dll+0xBB300`** = o chamador: `test al,al` → se for o socket do GS, ele **cifra o pacote** com a chave de 2 bytes do global `Main.dll+0xEB3F75/76`. **3) E o que o cliente enxergava** (li o `sockaddr` na memória depois da chamada): ``` 02 00 | AD 7B | 7F 00 00 01 → AF_INET | porta 44411 | IP 127.0.0.1 ``` **O peer é o NOSSO LOOPBACK.** Como o byp redireciona o connect para `127.0.0.1:<porta real + base>`, o cliente vê: - **base 0** → `127.0.0.1:45901` → a porta **coincide** com a real → passa *por acaso* ✓ - **base 100** → `127.0.0.1:46001` → **não coincide** → a validação local falha → o cliente não segue → **DC na seleção de sala** E o `main.exe+0x414BE0` (seleção de sala) tem o guard `cmp [ecx+0x30A4],0` → se a tabela local de 21 salas × 224 bytes não casar, ele **não envia nada** e fica esperando. ## Correção que fiz (byp novo, já compilado e deployado) **O proxy …\n- **Tools:** mcp_tool, search_files, edit_file, run_command, manage_todos","vector":[0.05264867,0.061505392,-0.1436931,-0.012882184,0.04552576,-0.0635112,0.023098992,0.0069191465,-0.028876109,-0.019256758,-0.0071552987,0.017413504,0.059340704,-0.03891914,0.004853834,-0.05920661,-0.022232179,-0.036215447,-0.032558445,-0.011603436,0.019772448,-0.033993304,-0.01678884,-0.036334053,0.11867036,0.025237782,0.025824303,0.06116346,-0.054314926,0.029348701,0.017815527,-0.011364331,-0.00072853535,-0.042020887,-0.007478166,-0.049