2464,0.002217723,-0.024532588,0.001338091,-0.0028895529,-0.027485602,-0.026567541,0.009059875,-0.064617634,-0.033186328,0.03361129,0.03179844,-0.025238032,-0.008595957,0.025860827,-0.030291563,0.036860365,-0.01977237,0.061999146,-0.02258946,0.015065318,-0.0028799106,-0.008813608,-0.031001328,0.0075200046,-0.046535727,0.017843604,-0.066993594,0.04790011,0.015779963,0.01383267,0.03998052,-0.04970811,0.013336202,-0.0033450422,0.051189497,0.02794565,-0.04830009,-0.02278266,0.00944653,0.020611718,-0.007930297,-0.021282211,0.040415194,0.008339142,-0.022390854,-0.040628154,0.004083372,0.013600234,0.0115541825,0.0024753716,-0.03785128,-0.008096051,-0.041479703,-0.026127664,-0.06508523,0.008445169,0.0105049405,0.030107353,-0.00050456903,-0.0021009878,0.028657364,0.011647679,-0.00084093923,-0.004687315,0.06546441,-0.012832543,-0.03959423,0.054802176,-0.025973547,-0.0001142883,0.025993995,0.041602463,0.11735459,0.033351604,-0.008940884,-0.08525576,0.043189052,0.010958712,0.014567691,-0.032226313,-0.019418636,-0.0010149158],"hash":"a562053a:1744","model":"nomic-embed-text:latest","archived":true},{"uri":"file:///f%3A/blackrockmu/.novamind/memory/session-log.md","text":"## 2026-11-16T22:53:22.037Z\n- **User:** sendo assim fixe o mutex local quero que vc fixe ele, da um nop nesse mutex prejudica? se nao faca, ou bypasse ele\n- **Outcome:** Feito e validado em runtime. ## Resposta curta: NOP no mutex **não** prejudica — mas NOP no lugar errado sim `OpenMutexA` é `__stdcall`: **a própria API limpa os 12 bytes dos argumentos**. Se você NOPar a *chamada*, esses 12 bytes ficam na pilha. O patch do `FindWindowA` que já temos tem exatamente esse efeito colateral (8 bytes) e só não quebra porque a função tem frame pointer (`push ebp; mov ebp,esp` em `Main.dll+0x13E680`) e o epílogo recompõe o `ESP` a partir do `EBP` — mas isso depende do build. Por isso eu fiz **NOP no salto**, não na chamada: a `call [OpenMutexA]` continua sendo feita (e continua limpando a pilha normalmente), e o cliente simplesmente **nunca salta para o `ExitProcess`**. Zero bytes mudam de lugar na pilha. ## O que o `Main.dll` realmente faz (build BlackrockMU, desmontei do binário) ``` 13E6A2 test byte [esi+4], 1 ; LauncherType & 1 13E6A7 jz 13E6BE ; bit1 OFF -> CAI DIRETO no bloco do mutex 13E6A9 push <\"Blackrock MU Online\"> 13E6B0 call [FindWindowA] ; <- patch que já existia 13E6B6 test eax,eax 13E6B8 jz 13E610 ; NÃO achou -> pula o bloco do mutex 13E6BE test byte [esi+4], 2 ; LauncherType & 2 13E6C1 jz 13E703 ; bit2 OFF -> pula o bloco 13E6C3 wsprintf(buff,\"XTEAM_MAIN_10405_%s\", IpAddress) 13E6E6 call [OpenMutexA] ; <- alvo do patch novo 13E6EC test eax,eax 13E6EE jnz 13E7E2 ; -> push 0 ; call [ExitProcess] (SAÍDA SILENCIOSA) 13E6F4 ... call [CreateMutexA] ... 13E7E2 push 0 ; call [ExitProcess] ``` Ou seja: **o bloco do mutex tem dois caminhos de…\n- **Tools:** search_files, read_file, manage_todos, edit_file, desktop_list_windows, desktop_snapshot, tool_search, run_command","vector":[0.089377195,0.032723058,-0.1439227,-0.045270193,0.02525103,-0.06805716,0.015599526,0.030227829,-0.0079182545,-0.0062245554,-0.06742389,0.030645099,0.0183557,0.014115199,0.027021049,-0.02059648,0.0078065093,-0.09236811,-0.04934373,0.016434953,0.0511562,-0.000022448869,-0.053066738,-0.020252366,0.07373019,0.045378808,0.051392466,0.038398214,-0.041485436,-0.0066302842,0.06391488,-0.010007344,0.016672926,-0.01940242,-0.016430723,-0.014203333,0.021957198,-0.008680359,-0.038492,0.023265796,0.015478302,-0.082580015,-0.012700252,-0.036794305,0.045971684,-0.013271688,0.057594832,0.0340124,0.040063925,-0.1354074,0.030596593,-0.008309867,0.015068663,-0.04289193,0.040740672,0.017472768,-0.024621814,0.077601425,0.015653677,0.020470174,0.07745166,0.052431386,-0.01299847,0.08579552,-0.008197456,0.014006549,-0.060730174,-0.005441123,-0.0297251,-0.014395578,0.04801069,0.023058916,-0.0064651803,-0.019441666,-0.014498094,-0.008120825,-0.033177506,-0.018088834,-0.046050556,-0.03019237,0.034580022,0.02172123,0.1